  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Rusty woodwart is a wood-decaying fungus in the Hypoxylaceae family that forms distinctive crusty, rusty-colored fruiting bodies on dead hardwood. It plays an important ecological role in breaking down woody material and nutrient cycling in New England forests.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Rusty coloration",
      "description": "Distinctive rust or reddish-brown colored crusty surface on wood, often powdery or granular in texture",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Fruiting body structure",
      "description": "Forms hard, charcoal-like stromata that feel brittle and can be scraped to reveal black interior tissue",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Host preference",
      "description": "Grows on dead or dying hardwood logs and branches, particularly oak and other deciduous trees",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Rusty woodwart inhabits moist, shaded areas of New England forests where dead hardwood logs and fallen branches accumulate on the forest floor.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its crusty, uninviting appearance, rusty woodwart produces ascospores within its hard fruiting body that are forcibly ejected into the air when conditions are wet, allowing them to spread and colonize new wood sources.",
